per now i feel  really nice and i have also  the flexibility to move with the support  which is  pretty comfortable  depending on the sport you might also  put some gripper underneath support  that would also help you could also even  put it where your chest touches the  guitar there's so many different options  but you can't go wrong with some gripper  that's really useful  another great trick that i don't see  almost anyone utilizing  is to take advantage of these klingon  stabilizers these are this little static  electricity stabilizers you can take  them off the sheet  and basically all you do is you put them  on the instrument where the wood touches  your thigh a lot of my loots especially  i find this so useful because the loots  have a round back and they're famously  slippery  but you can put this also on your guitar  even again it doesn't hurt your guitar i  put it on right there  and then  you instantly have that extra grip and  you don't have to carry around a gripper  so  very useful  all right we have one more support  device today the biggest  the biggest of the day this is a really  cool look and support by the company  guitar lift also made in germany this  guitar lift  kind of struck me as  too big and bulky to carry around when i  first saw it and i didn't really know  what to expect  it uses suction cups like the ergo play  but i have to say i was blown away in  the first two seconds because there was  no assembly uh i just took it out of the  box  and i just put the  thing on my thigh there's kind of like a  foam pad where your thigh goes  i put it there i just held up the guitar  where i wanted it  and then from here you just lean the  guitar into the suction cups  and  let go  so i literally this thing arrived in a  package i opened the box i took it off  and i did that and in two seconds uh i  was holding the guitar where i wanted it  to go  so in terms of ease this beats all the  other devices you really can't go wrong  so uh i'm pretty impressed  there are four suction cups which go  against the wood and again don't worry  it doesn't hurt your instrument what i'm  noticing is that the guitar lift itself  is pretty heavy and so my guitar becomes  quite heavy uh even though it's a very  light instrument so i'm not sure how  much i like that although when you put  it against your your leg and it falls in  all that weight falls into your leg it  does feel very secure  so i think the major thing i left about  this support is that  it's super convenient easy to install  and i really don't even feel the need to  add a gripper because it's uh it's  really not going anywhere but it is a  suction cup device so so maybe one will  come off in the middle and you have to  lean against it so again i've only been  using this one and the wood side support  for a few weeks  but it's pretty impressive  [Music]  all right so we tried all these  different guitar supports which ones you  to go with  i think in the end  there's no perfect support there's no  one way to hold the guitar  they all have pros and they all have  cons  the table did support the instrument but  it was really terribly inconvenient  the strap is very convenient and cheap  and also you could even stand when you  play which is really nice but i have to  admit that when i wear a strap i  sometimes feel kind of straight jacket  kind of tied to my instrument and i i  can't move my body free from the  instrument so that's a con foot stools  are super portable they're easy to carry  around throw in your guitar case it's a  traditional way of holding the guitar  but i don't find it ergonomic the ergo  play is really convenient lightweight  super adjustable it puts the guitar  right where i want it  but sometimes the suction cups come off  the wood side guitar support was great  for flexibility of motion  but there was some assembly required and  to get exactly the right height uh took  a little bit of time and lastly the  guitar lift was like two seconds out 